    "hardest_part": "Surviving the Death world early game long enough to bootstrap a self-sustaining iron line: hand-mining enough iron/stone/coal, hand-crafting a burner drill plus stone furnaces, and keeping the character alive with a pistol against escalating biter waves, all while every single action costs a full step (one input per second of host time).",
    "likely_failure": "Character death to a biter swarm (or running dry on ammunition while kiting) before the burner-drill/furnace setup is defended, plus wasted steps from imprecise pointer targeting (mis-clicking ore tiles, furnace/drill placement, or inventory slots) that stall the manual mining and smelting chain; a secondary failure is never reaching a lab plus steady red-science throughput because gears/copper logistics eat all remaining time.",
    "helpful_mechanics": "Hand mining (J) and hand crafting work from the first second with no infrastructure, so iron-ore, iron-plate and gear-wheel milestones are reachable almost immediately; burner drills and stone furnaces need no electricity and feed each other directly, letting automated_iron accumulate passively while other tasks are done; milestones are cumulative relative to baseline so every single ore/plate counts, and the game is paused while I think, so only my own action steps advance the threat clock.",
    "harmful_mechanics": "Death world settings (fast evolution, aggressive expansion, frequent attacks) combined with marathon recipe costs make every building expensive and every idle second dangerous; only one input executes per step with a one-second host-time cost, so walking, aiming and mining all consume the limited budget; no power infrastructure or defenses at spawn, and native character death ends the attempt, wiping all progress."
